            "text_G4bG4k": {
              "type": "text",
              "name": "t:names.description",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<p>Prissie Pratt is an artist of rare consistency, in that her subject has never once wavered. It is herself: studied, staged, doubted, corrected, and returned to with a frequency that suggests both discipline and mild obsession. We meet at Onyx coffee which might be the greatest coffee I've ever had - and I'm in Arkansas.</p><p>Her paintings and mixed-media works occupy that precarious space between glamour and grievance. Pratt appears somewhat unhinged, less as a narcissistic exercise than as an ongoing negotiation with her reflection. I question if she is not merely looking at herself, but checking to see whether she is still being looked at - all the time in fact - our 3 lattes proves this theory as she perseverates on her reflection in the shops' window.</p><p>Formally, she draws from the expected sources. In her exact words: \"London art school theory, TikTok aesthetics, and the low-grade hysteria of contemporary cultural art-show life is my inspiration.\" What distinguishes the work is not originality of reference, but insistence. The images are meticulously constructed, faintly exhausted, and entirely aware of both facts.</p><p>Pratt's studio habits are, by contrast, strangely domestic. She begins most mornings with soft scrambled eggs and Marmite, an iced coffee diluted to near uselessness, and a slice of toast she rarely finishes. This routine, she insists, is coincidental. It clearly is not. She dislikes working past mid-afternoon, is suspicious of artists who claim to enjoy early mornings, and considers minor irritation a legitimate research tool.</p><p>Critics have labelled her self-obsessed work \"as interesting,\" two words she oddly obsesses over- like a bee returning to the same flower after the nectar is clearly drained. Mildly accurate. The work amuses those who sit with the narrative. The obsession is not with self-admiration, but with self-maintenance: how one remains coherent, visible, and relevant while perpetually on display.</p><p>Prissie Pratt eats her breakfast in Bentonville.</p><p>xoHenri Vausselin, Art Critic and Cigar Connoisseur</p>",
                "width": "100%",
                "max_width": "none",
                "alignment": "center",
                "type_preset": "rte",
                "font": "var(--font-body--family)",
                "font_size": "1rem",
